      <section>
        <h2>The Key Distinction: Seat Width vs. Seat Depth</h2>
        <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. One thing that became clear while researching chairs is that "wide seat" means different things to different users, and the common confusion between seat width and seat depth causes a lot of misguided purchasing decisions.</p>

        <p><strong>Seat width</strong> is the left-to-right measurement of the seat pan. It determines hip clearance — whether you can sit without your hips touching the armrests or seat edges. The ergonomic recommendation is roughly 1–2 inches of clearance on each side of the hip. For most users, 18"–20" is sufficient. Users in the 95th+ percentile for hip width may need 20"–22".</p>

        <p><strong>Seat depth</strong> is the front-to-back measurement. It determines thigh clearance — whether the seat can support your thigh without pressing into the back of your knee. This is the dimension that tall users primarily struggle with. Tall frames have longer femurs, and a seat that is too shallow leaves the knee unsupported, transferring load onto the popliteal area and causing leg fatigue and numbness.</p>

        <p>Most tall users who describe wanting a "wider chair" actually need a deeper chair. If you are looking for a chair because your current one leaves your thighs unsupported or your knees hang off the edge, you need more seat depth — not seat width. If you are looking because the armrests press into your hips or the sides of the seat feel constrictive, that is a seat width problem.</p>

        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm">
          <strong>Note on testing:</strong> I personally use the Steelcase Gesture. Other chairs in this guide are research-based assessments from manufacturer specs, engineering analysis, and community reports.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What Seat Width Do You Actually Need?</h2>
        <p>The biomechanical sizing formula for seat width is hip width plus approximately 2–4 inches for clearance. Male hip width in the 95th percentile is approximately 16"–17" — meaning a seat width of 18"–21" covers virtually all users on hip width alone.</p>
        <p>The users who genuinely need a wide seat — above 20" — are those with hip widths above the 95th percentile or those who find standard seat widths constrictive due to how their weight distributes in a seated position. Tall users as a group do not necessarily have wider hips than shorter users; height and width are partially independent body dimensions.</p>
        <p>Before focusing on seat width, verify these specs first (in priority order for tall users):</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Seat height maximum — must reach your required seated height</li>
          <li>Seat depth (adjustable preferred) — must cover your thigh length</li>
          <li>Seat width — verify it is at least 18" and not constrictive for your frame</li>
        </ol>
        <p>For height-specific seat height requirements, see the <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">correct chair dimensions guide →</a></p>
      </section>

