      <section>
        <h2>The Seat Width + Seat Depth Interaction</h2>
        <p>One reason wide seats are harder to find in ergonomic chairs is the seat width–depth tradeoff in chair engineering. Wider seat pans require wider base frames to maintain stability under load. Deeper seat pans extend the front edge further from the base. These two dimensions interact structurally — maximizing both simultaneously requires more complex manufacturing, which is why wide-and-deep chairs tend to be either budget-reinforced (La-Z-Boy Trafford) or premium-engineered (Leap Plus).</p>
        <p>The middle tier of ergonomic chairs (Sihoo Doro S300, Branch, Hbada) prioritizes seat height and back system quality at a specific price point and delivers an 18"–19" wide seat at a fixed 17"–18" depth. Neither dimension is exceptional, but both are adequate for the 6'0"–6'2" range — which is who these chairs are designed for.</p>
        <p>For users who need both wide and deep, the product landscape is narrow: the Leap Plus at the premium tier, or the La-Z-Boy Trafford at the budget tier (with the height and depth limitations noted).</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>What is the widest office chair available?</h3>
        <p>Among mainstream ergonomic chairs, the La-Z-Boy Trafford (~22" seat width) and Steelcase Leap Plus (~21") are the widest options that also address tall-user seat height. For users who are both tall and broad, the Leap Plus is the stronger recommendation because it also reaches 22.5" seat height and provides adjustable seat depth — the Trafford's seat height and depth constraints make it appropriate only through about 6'2".</p>

        <h3>How wide should an office chair seat be for broad-shouldered tall people?</h3>
        <p>Seat width is driven by hip width, not shoulder width. Shoulder width affects armrest width and back width, not the seat pan. Most users are comfortable in an 18"–20" seat width. If your hips feel constrained in a standard chair, look for 20"–22". If your shoulders are the issue, check armrest span and back width instead.</p>

        <h3>Does a wider seat help tall people fit better?</h3>
        <p>Not usually. Tall users primarily need more seat height and seat depth. Width is a secondary concern unless your frame is genuinely broader than average. Resolve height and depth requirements first — a wide chair that sits too low or has a seat too shallow to support your thighs is still a poor fit regardless of its lateral dimensions.</p>
